# BSL Mesh: public repository and publication safety

This project is publicly available at https://github.com/gesichtslos/bsl-mesh. Treat every commit, pull request, diff, CI log, artifact, issue, and release as readable by anyone and archived indefinitely. Open source is deliberate. Access to project secrets, organization data, users' data, and operating infrastructure is not.

Ordinary safe changes are committed and opened as pull requests without asking the owner to confirm publication. Ask only when publication rights are unclear, a concrete leak is suspected, or an action needs permissions this rule does not grant.

## 1. What may be published

- Reviewed source code, tests, reproducible synthetic fixtures, public architectural decisions, public API/MCP schemas, safe build scripts, project Rules and Skills, documentation, and examples that use obviously fake nonfunctional placeholders.
- Public infrastructure templates that contain no real credentials, internal hostnames, deployment tokens, private paths, or sensitive internal configuration.
- Vulnerability descriptions only under the project's disclosure policy. Do not publish an unpatched exploit or a live secret in an issue or pull request.

A license file is published only after the owner chooses one. Do not select a license on the owner's behalf.

## 2. What must never be committed or exposed

- Real credentials: `.env` with secrets, access tokens, private keys, SSH keys, API keys, passwords, OAuth secrets, session cookies, signing keys, certificates with private keys, tokens in URLs, or `.npmrc` tokens.
- Personal data, private correspondence, user submissions, employer or third-party BSL/1C source, and private client prompts or context.
- Real database dumps, backups, logs, traces, production configuration, internal endpoints, deployment keys, or CI artifacts that contain any of the above.
- Material taken from local files outside the repository only because the agent can read the filesystem.
- Unreviewed third-party code whose license or confidentiality is incompatible with publication.

README files, Markdown, test fixtures, generated files, GitHub Actions, Cursor Rules and Skills, lockfiles, comments, pull request text, and build logs are publication surfaces too. A placeholder must be obviously fake.

## 3. Before each commit, pull request, or push

1. Identify the exact files and whether their publication rights are known. Prefer `git add <paths>`. Inspect generated and untracked files.
2. Review `git status --short`, `git diff --check`, `git diff --cached --check`, `git diff --cached --name-status`, and the staged diff. For a push, review the whole commit range.
3. Confirm ignored sensitive files are not staged and were not copied into tracked files. `.gitignore` and `.cursorignore` are not security boundaries.
4. Run `node scripts/scan-secrets.mjs --staged` before committing. CI runs `node scripts/scan-secrets.mjs` on tracked files. Do not install Gitleaks or send the tree to an external scanner without the owner's approval. A clean scanner result is not proof that the diff is safe to publish. Review the content as well: scanners miss private business information.
5. Inspect `.github/workflows/`, Dockerfiles, Compose files, deployment scripts, dependency installation, and new Rules or Skills for hidden data transmission or elevated permissions.
6. Run the relevant tests. Report commands that actually ran and checks that did not.
7. If content or provenance is uncertain, leave that material out and tell the owner what was excluded. Continue the rest of the safe work. Do not stop an ordinary change to ask whether publishing the repository's own reviewed code is allowed.

## 4. GitHub and CI

- Use a feature branch and a pull request for nontrivial changes. Do not force-push or rewrite shared history without explicit authorization. Do not bypass failed checks or secret-scanning push protection.
- Ordinary test workflows use `permissions: contents: read`. Do not grant write permissions except in a separate job that needs them.
- External pull requests are untrusted. Test them with `pull_request`, not `pull_request_target`. Do not give a fork pull request secrets or production access.
- Do not print secrets or real environment values in logs. Public CI uses synthetic credentials only.
- Do not send repository contents to extra analytics, scanners, or LLM APIs without the owner's approval.

## 5. Possible accidental disclosure

- If the material is not published yet, remove it from the index and review the commit range again.
- If it is already pushed, do not treat a follow-up deletion as remediation. Tell the owner the paths and the kind of exposure. Do not repeat the secret.
- Rotate affected credentials with the owner before rewriting history. Do not force-push shared history alone.
- Add a regression check that uses synthetic data only.

## 6. What to report

For a publication, state the files reviewed, risks considered, scanner result, tests run, and whether a commit, push, or pull request happened. State that a clean scan is not proof of safety. This rule complements `docs/08-CLIENT-SECURITY-MANIFEST.md` and does not replace it.
